# Independently reviewed native stop fixture

This is a lead-operated non-managed fixture, not task50 producer code. It owns
no Kata issue, worker seat or native goal, and cannot accept a milestone.
All runtime effects wait for an immutable source commit, cold retention,
independent source review and explicit `NativeFixtureLaunchAdmission`.

`native_stop_fixture.py launch --authorization ABS --authorization-sha256 HEX
--output ABS` runs inside an explicitly admitted exclusive cgroup with at most
256 MiB RAM and zero swap. The admission pins source bytes, candidate, independent
reviewer thread, retention, user authority, socket/peer/daemon incarnation,
operator scope, separate fixture workspace/common Git directory and fresh output.
The fixed command digest pins its own immutable payload source and owned output.
Launch reopens actual Git facts from a clean linked worktree on the unique
`codex/collaboration50-fixture/<fixture-id>` branch at the admitted base,
checks directory incarnations and rejects canonical/source/output overlap.
Git, Python and systemd-run use absolute digest-pinned executables; payload Python
uses isolated mode. Each Upgrade/request/frame has a whole-operation deadline,
so a peer dribbling bytes cannot extend a request beyond its admitted timeout.

Only `thread/start` and `thread/shellCommand` mutate native state. The former
creates one fresh no-goal thread with `approvalPolicy=never`, explicit
`danger-full-access` and cooperative workspace roots.
`environments` is omitted: the pinned native `ThreadStartParams` schema says
omission selects the default execution environment, while an empty array
disables environment access. The first live fixture used that empty array and
entered `systemError` without a command or payload witness; its original effects
and failed readbacks remain retained. A correction uses a fresh separately
admitted fixture, never a retry or cleanup of that original thread.
The latter sends one fixed
command with a 90-second native timeout. The command creates a separate 128 MiB,
zero-swap scope; its single Python payload records an actual live process and
cgroup witness, waits 60 seconds and returns. No subprocess, Git/source/scientific
write, model turn or daemon stop is part of the payload. Native shell commands
are explicitly unsandboxed; they do not prove model-worker permission grants.
The operator keeps its connection for another 95 seconds. Every request is
durable before send, raw matching responses are retained, and uncertainty never
licenses a mutation retry. During those same 95 seconds, a passive bounded read
on the original connection retains only the fixture's own public turn/status
and command-item start/completion metadata. Command text becomes a digest;
output, private reasoning, other threads and other item types are discarded.
No application RPC or subscription is added. WebSocket ping responses remain
protocol control traffic. The diagnostic has a 128-frame cap, 32 MiB retained
output cap and at most two seconds to finish a started frame; idle waiting does
not extend the 95-second window. EOF, a partial-frame timeout or overflow remains
UNKNOWN without retry, release or cleanup. These projected events diagnose a
read-surface coverage gap; they are not accepted witness or stop receipts.
The longer bounded live interval allows the observer to reopen both cold source
packages before collecting its nonempty witness: measured operator materialization
and retention checks took 7.620 and 1.340 seconds. Observer/host deadline remains
120 seconds; resource caps and the two observations at least five seconds apart
remain unchanged.

The shell RPC acknowledgement contains no returned turn ID. The independently
admitted A50 observer must derive the actual fresh turn/item/handle identity from
complete native lists and match the literal command digest and live process
witness. `fixture-launch.json` is raw provenance, not a stop receipt. Neither
the acknowledgement nor `operator-returning.json` proves terminal tools, empty
queue, quiescence, cleanup or retirement. Missing native association/coverage is
a retained blocker; no invented `turn/start` receipt or scan-only fallback exists.

Separate model-worker/common-Git/nested-Insula capability and isolated Kata
proofs remain required before task50 producer implementation/managed effects.
Changing transport framing preserves the full original stop/queue/tool/process
contract, independent Insula gates, budgets and historical authorization pins.

## Revision2 reconciliation (fresh-source admission pending)

The normative revision2 contract and exact independent review are pinned in
`docs/superpowers/plans/2026-10-04-native-stop-observation-reconciliation.md`.
They supersede the diagnostic-only event treatment above for one fresh,
separately admitted fixture. An initial witness may conjoin an original-channel
start event, six complete native read results and a contemporaneous live kernel
witness. An empty native item list remains empty. Both terminal observations
still require the actual complete terminal item history with exact command IDs
and integer exit codes; completion events cannot replace those lists.

`native_stop_channel.py` captures every complete frame from arm in a consecutive
hash-linked prefix. Actual socket drains establish bounded coverage cutoffs and
preserve peer identity; partial frames, unsupported owned tools, gaps, overflows,
EOF or unidentified writers remain UNKNOWN. Capture output uses a fresh sibling
directory outside the fixture, workspace, common Git, canonical, source, cold
and observer roots. No writer exemptions are introduced. After two independently
retained terminal observations at least five seconds apart, a capture-only
close request closes the passive channel. A closed channel alone proves no stop:
the observer also reopens the final prefix and actual reaped recorder command,
birth readback, full source and resource observations before sealing.

`preflight_driver.py` is fixed to static, observer and operator roles. It owns no
task, claim, model turn or acceptance oracle. Immutable host intents precede
spawn; actual actor birth, argv, scope and original clock survive exec. Separate
static preparation and live commands each retain their original120s bound,
256MiB limit and zero swap. Actual ObserverReady acknowledgement precedes sole
thread creation; it must remain at most two seconds old at send. Timeout or
uncertainty never permits a kill, mutation retry, ownership release or cleanup.

The exact Python interpreter and source import closure are reopened. Two
readonly executions of the reviewed `python_import_profile.py`, using `-B` and
`-I -B`, derive exact bootstrap module names, observed import paths, resolved
regular file targets and hashes. This captures the installed `sitecustomize`
alias without granting a site-directory or symlink wildcard. Both raw command
outputs, flags, source, clocks and exit codes must match the admitted profile;
any different import alias or body refuses before native effects. The profile
cannot admit task50 or repair earlier failed fixture receipts.
